const e=[{slug:"property-scams-buying-abroad",title:"How to spot a property scam when you buy a house abroad",desc:"The warning signs we see in cheap-house listings, and six checks that stop most scams before you send any money.",date:"2026-10-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/DcoUnwBDDxF-1.webp",tags:["guide","safety"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Cheap houses attract buyers from far away, and that is exactly what scammers count on: you cannot easily visit, you do not know the local rules, and a good price makes you want to move fast.</p>
<h2>Warning signs</h2>
<ul><li>The price is far below every similar home in the same town, with no reason given (ruin, auction, shared ownership).</li>
<li>The "owner" lives abroad, cannot show the house and asks you to pay a deposit to "reserve" it.</li>
<li>You are asked to pay by bank transfer to a personal account, by crypto, or through a money-transfer service.</li>
<li>The photos appear on other listings with a different address or price. A reverse image search takes a minute.</li>
<li>Pressure: "three other buyers are interested", "the offer ends today".</li>
<li>No land-registry or cadastral reference, or the seller refuses to give one.</li></ul>
<h2>Six checks before you pay anything</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Get the registry extract.</strong> In Spain the nota simple, in Italy the visura catastale, in France the extrait cadastral. It shows who owns the house and whether there are debts on it.</li>
<li><strong>Check the owner's name matches the seller.</strong> If an agent sells, ask for the signed mandate from the owner.</li>
<li><strong>Use your own lawyer or notary,</strong> not one recommended by the seller.</li>
<li><strong>Pay only into an escrow or a notary's client account,</strong> never to a person.</li>
<li><strong>Visit, or send someone.</strong> A local surveyor or a video call walking through every room is enough to prove the house exists and matches the photos.</li>
<li><strong>Read the reservation contract</strong> before you sign: what happens to your deposit if the sale fails.</li></ol>
<h2>What RealHunt does</h2>
<p>A person checks every home before it goes up, and we link to the original listing so you deal with the real seller or agent. That is not a guarantee: if something on a listing looks wrong, use the report button on its page and we check it.</p>
<p>This is general information, not legal advice. Before you buy, get a local lawyer or notary.</p>`},{slug:"viewing-checklist-cheap-house",title:"What to check when you visit a cheap house: a 20-point checklist",desc:"Roof, damp, water, power, access, paperwork: the things that turn a cheap house into an expensive one, and how to check them in one visit.",date:"2026-10-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/DdSm-JkiJem-1.webp",tags:["guide","buying"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Most cheap houses are cheap because of something you can see on a visit, if you know where to look. Take this list with you, or send it to the person who visits for you.</p>
<h2>Outside</h2>
<ol><li>Roof: missing or slipped tiles, sagging lines, stains on the ceiling below.</li>
<li>Walls: cracks wider than a coin, especially diagonal ones from window corners.</li>
<li>Damp: a white salt line or flaking paint at the bottom of walls.</li>
<li>Drainage: where rain water goes, and whether the ground slopes toward the house.</li>
<li>Access: can a car or a van reach the door all year? Who owns the track?</li>
<li>Boundaries: does the plot on the ground match the plot on the cadastral map?</li></ol>
<h2>Inside</h2>
<ol start="7"><li>Smell: a musty smell means damp, even if the walls were just painted.</li>
<li>Electricity: age of the fuse box, earthing, whether the supply is connected or cut off.</li>
<li>Water: mains, well or tank; pressure at the top tap; hot water.</li>
<li>Waste water: mains sewer or septic tank, and whether the tank meets current rules.</li>
<li>Heating: what kind, what it costs a winter, and whether the chimney works.</li>
<li>Windows: single or double glazing, rot in wooden frames.</li>
<li>Floors: soft spots, slopes, woodworm holes in beams.</li>
<li>Kitchen and bathroom: usable now, or a full rebuild?</li></ol>
<h2>Around</h2>
<ol start="15"><li>Phone signal and internet: test it in the house, not in the village square.</li>
<li>Noise: visit once on a weekday and once in the evening if you can.</li>
<li>Neighbours: empty houses next door can mean damp or structural problems shared through walls.</li>
<li>Services: nearest shop, doctor and pharmacy, and how long the drive is in winter.</li></ol>
<h2>Paperwork</h2>
<ol start="19"><li>The registry extract matches the house you saw: rooms, size, outbuildings. Unregistered extensions can be a problem when you sell.</li>
<li>Energy certificate, and any pending local taxes or community fees.</li></ol>
<p>If several of these need work, get a quote before you make an offer: it is your best argument for a lower price. RealHunt's <a href="/help-buying">help buying</a> page lists local surveyors and lawyers.</p>`},{slug:"bali-phuket-tulum-batumi-400-new-builds-scored",title:"Bali, Phuket, Tulum or Batumi? 400 new builds scored side by side",desc:"Median price, price per m², distance to the sea and facilities for 400 new-build projects. Batumi is the cheapest by far; Tulum is the furthest from the beach.",date:"2026-10-06",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/top/batumi/status-house-by-elt-building-batumi-boulevard-batumi-0.webp",tags:["rankings","data"],minutes:4,html:`<p>We keep a Top 100 of new-build projects in four places where foreigners buy holiday and rental homes: Bali, Phuket, Tulum and Batumi. This month we gave each of the 400 projects ten scores from 0 to 100. Here is what the four markets look like side by side.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Median price</th><th>Median €/m²</th><th>Under €100k</th><th>Median distance to sea</th><th>Within 1 km of sea</th><th>Ready now</th><th>Pool</th><th>Gym</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="/top/batumi">Batumi</a></td><td>€48,630</td><td>€1,358</td><td>88</td><td>650 m</td><td>73</td><td>34</td><td>56</td><td>44</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/top/bali">Bali</a></td><td>€153,555</td><td>€1,871</td><td>24</td><td>1.5 km</td><td>41</td><td>64</td><td>93</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/top/tulum">Tulum</a></td><td>€146,927</td><td>€2,835</td><td>9</td><td>2.7 km</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>98</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/top/phuket">Phuket</a></td><td>€159,134</td><td>€3,658</td><td>14</td><td>1.0 km</td><td>50</td><td>45</td><td>94</td><td>59</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Counts are out of 100 projects in each ranking.</p><h2>Cheapest: Batumi, by far</h2><p>The median Batumi project starts at €48,630, about a third of the other three. 88 of its 100 projects start under €100,000, against 24 in Bali, 14 in Phuket and 9 in Tulum. Most are apartments in towers along the seafront, and 73 are within 1 km of the sea.</p><h2>Most expensive per m²: Phuket</h2><p>Phuket's median is about €3,658 per m², almost double Bali (€1,871). In exchange, Phuket buildings list the most facilities after Bali: 94 have a pool and 59 a gym.</p><h2>Furthest from the beach: Tulum</h2><p>The median Tulum project is 2.7 km from the sea in a straight line, and only 6 of 100 are within 1 km. Most new building is inland in La Veleta, Aldea Zama and Region 15. Tulum is also almost all off-plan: 1 of 100 is ready today, so the developer's track record matters more there than anywhere else.</p><h2>Best balance: Bali</h2><p>Bali has 64 projects ready to move in, 41 within 1 km of the sea and the richest facilities: 93 pools, 64 gyms and 32 coworking spaces. Check the ownership: most foreigners buy leasehold there, and the listing does not always say which it is.</p><h2>Pick yours</h2><p>Every ranking can be filtered and sorted by the ten scores: <a href="/top/bali">Bali</a>, <a href="/top/phuket">Phuket</a>, <a href="/top/tulum">Tulum</a>, <a href="/top/batumi">Batumi</a>. We wrote one article per destination too: <a href="/blog/best-new-builds-bali-scored-2026">Bali</a>, <a href="/blog/best-new-builds-phuket-scored-2026">Phuket</a>, <a href="/blog/best-new-builds-tulum-scored-2026">Tulum</a> and <a href="/blog/best-new-builds-batumi-scored-2026">Batumi</a>.</p><h2>How the scores work</h2><p>Every project gets ten scores from 0 to 100: price, value per m², location, beach, facilities, delivery, developer, space, visitor interest and listing detail. The grade of each project is the plain average of the ten. Price, value, space and visitor interest compare a project with the other 99 in the same ranking. Beach uses the straight-line distance to the coastline on OpenStreetMap, and facilities counts what the developer's public listing shows (pool, gym, spa, coworking, security and so on). Those two use the same scale in every destination, so you can compare Bali with Batumi.</p><p>None of this is advice to buy. Prices and dates come from public listings and change. Ask the developer before you decide, and check the facilities are built, not only drawn.</p>`},{slug:"best-new-builds-bali-scored-2026",title:"Best new builds in Bali, scored: price, beach and facilities (2026)",desc:"We scored the 100 projects in our Bali Top 100 from 0 to 100 on price, beach, facilities and seven more things. Median price €153,555; 41 are within 1 km of the sea.",date:"2026-10-06",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/top/bali/grey-stone-villa-ungasan-bali-0.webp",tags:["rankings","bali"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Bali is the most searched of our four rankings, and the hardest to compare: villas, lofts and apartments, freehold and leasehold, rice fields and cliff tops. So we scored all 100 projects in our Bali Top 100 on the same ten things.</p><p>The median project starts at €153,555, at about €1,871 per m². 24 of the 100 start under €100,000, 41 are within 1 km of the sea and 64 are ready to move in today. 93 list a pool, 64 a gym and 32 a coworking space.</p><h2>The top 5 overall</h2><ul><li>#1 <a href="/top/bali/the-umalas-signature-bali">The Umalas Signature</a>, Umalas: from €143,550, grade 83/100, 1.5 km to the sea</li><li>#2 <a href="/top/bali/grey-stone-villa-ungasan-bali">Grey Stone Villa</a>, Ungasan, Uluwatu: from €143,550, grade 82/100, 1.5 km to the sea</li><li>#3 <a href="/top/bali/plagoo-residence-benoa-bali">Plagoo Residence</a>, Benoa, Nusa Dua: from €74,213, grade 80/100, 1.3 km to the sea</li><li>#4 <a href="/top/bali/inika-seaside-denpasar-bali">Inika Seaside</a>, Denpasar: from €70,157, grade 80/100, 300 m to the sea</li><li>#5 <a href="/top/bali/the-springs-jimbaran-bali">The Springs</a>, Jimbaran: from €85,782, grade 76/100, 1.1 km to the sea</li></ul><h2>Closest to the sea</h2><p>Measured in a straight line to the coastline, so the walk is a bit longer.</p><ul><li><a href="/top/bali/bocoa-jimbaran-bali">BOCOA Jimbaran</a>, Jimbaran: 150 m, from €205,320 (#37)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/bingin-villas-bali">Bingin Villas</a>, Bingin, Uluwatu: 250 m, from €201,840 (#9)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/surfside-pecatu-bali">Surfside</a>, Pecatu, Uluwatu: 250 m, from €145,290 (#92)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/the-sand-villas-sanur-bali">The Sand Villas</a>, Sanur: 250 m, from €182,242 (#74)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/desa-harmonis-uluwatu-bali">Desa Harmonis Uluwatu</a>, Uluwatu: 300 m, from €130,500 (#56)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/bali?sort=sea">See all Bali projects sorted by distance to the sea</a></p><h2>Best facilities</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/bali/oxo-townhouses-berawa-bali">OXO Townhouses Berawa</a>, Berawa, Canggu: pool, gym, coworking, rooftop, security, parking, garden. From €243,600</li><li><a href="/top/bali/bingin-villas-bali">Bingin Villas</a>, Bingin, Uluwatu: pool, gym, spa, rooftop, security, parking, garden. From €201,840</li><li><a href="/top/bali/hideaway-villas-uluwatu-bali">Hideaway Villas Uluwatu</a>, Uluwatu: pool, gym, spa, coworking, security, parking, garden. From €165,300</li><li><a href="/top/bali/budda-club-house-north-kuta-bali">Budda Club House</a>, North Kuta: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, security, parking. From €75,690</li><li><a href="/top/bali/kiara-ocean-place-north-kuta-bali">Kiara Ocean Place</a>, North Kuta: pool, gym, spa, coworking, security, parking, garden. From €152,250</li></ul><p><a href="/top/bali?sort=facilities">Sort the Bali Top 100 by facilities</a> or pick the ones you need, for example <a href="/top/bali?amen=pool,gym">pool and gym</a>.</p><h2>Cheapest entry prices</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/bali/ubud-dream-bali">Ubud Dream</a>, Ubud: from €56,550, about €2,154 per m² (#86)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/vodopad-ubud-bali">Vodopad</a>, Ubud: from €58,290, about €2,082 per m² (#89)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/tao-club-house-pererenan-bali">TAO Club House</a>, Pererenan, Canggu: from €62,640, about €2,237 per m² (#68)</li><li><a href="/top/bali/habbi-melasti-residence-bali">Habbi Melasti Residence</a>, Melasti, Uluwatu: from €68,904, about €1,863 per m² (#98)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/bali?max=100000">All projects under €100,000 in Bali</a></p><h2>How the scores work</h2><p>Every project gets ten scores from 0 to 100: price, value per m², location, beach, facilities, delivery, developer, space, visitor interest and listing detail. The grade of each project is the plain average of the ten. Price, value, space and visitor interest compare a project with the other 99 in the same ranking. Beach uses the straight-line distance to the coastline on OpenStreetMap, and facilities counts what the developer's public listing shows (pool, gym, spa, coworking, security and so on). Those two use the same scale in every destination, so you can compare Bali with Batumi.</p><p>None of this is advice to buy. Prices and dates come from public listings and change. Ask the developer before you decide, and check the facilities are built, not only drawn.</p>`},{slug:"best-new-builds-phuket-scored-2026",title:"Best new builds in Phuket, scored: price, beach and facilities (2026)",desc:"We scored the 100 projects in our Phuket Top 100 from 0 to 100 on price, beach, facilities and seven more things. Median price €159,134; 50 are within 1 km of the sea.",date:"2026-10-06",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/top/phuket/patong-bay-hill-phuket-0.webp",tags:["rankings","phuket"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Phuket has the most expensive square metre of our four rankings, but also some of the best-equipped buildings. We scored the 100 projects in our Phuket Top 100 on the same ten things so you can see where the money goes.</p><p>The median project starts at €159,134, at about €3,658 per m². 14 of the 100 start under €100,000, 50 are within 1 km of the sea and 45 are ready to move in today. 94 list a pool, 59 a gym and 7 a coworking space.</p><h2>The top 5 overall</h2><ul><li>#1 <a href="/top/phuket/wallaya-residence-nai-harn-rawai-phuket">Wallaya Residence Nai Harn</a>, Rawai: from €113,445, grade 84/100, 1.0 km to the sea</li><li>#2 <a href="/top/phuket/patong-bay-hill-phuket">Patong Bay Hill</a>, Patong: from €117,910, grade 83/100, 1.4 km to the sea</li><li>#3 <a href="/top/phuket/vega-condominium-layan-phuket">Vega Condominium</a>, Layan: from €84,821, grade 79/100, 2.5 km to the sea</li><li>#4 <a href="/top/phuket/rawayana-beachfront-village-rawai-phuket">Rawayana Beachfront Village</a>, Rawai: from €132,878, grade 79/100, 0 m to the sea</li><li>#5 <a href="/top/phuket/the-one-nai-harn-phuket">The One</a>, Nai Harn: from €85,793, grade 77/100, 1.0 km to the sea</li></ul><h2>Closest to the sea</h2><p>Measured in a straight line to the coastline, so the walk is a bit longer.</p><ul><li><a href="/top/phuket/rawayana-beachfront-village-rawai-phuket">Rawayana Beachfront Village</a>, Rawai: 0 m, from €132,878 (#4)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/twinpalms-residences-kamala-phuket">Twinpalms Residences</a>, Kamala: 50 m, from €709,034 (#27)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/mgallery-residences-kamala-phuket">MGallery Residences</a>, Kamala: 100 m, from €233,427 (#31)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/bellevue-beachfront-condo-layan-phuket">Bellevue Beachfront Condo</a>, Layan: 150 m, from €147,059 (#24)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/coral-beach-oceanview-resort-patong-phuket">Coral Beach Oceanview Resort Patong</a>, Patong: 200 m, from €227,679 (#14)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/phuket?sort=sea">See all Phuket projects sorted by distance to the sea</a></p><h2>Best facilities</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/phuket/the-one-nai-harn-phuket">The One</a>, Nai Harn: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, restaurant or bar, security. From €85,793</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/the-sunny-moon-nai-harn-phuket">The Sunny Moon</a>, Nai Harn: pool, gym, spa, coworking, restaurant or bar, security, parking. From €153,072</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/atrium-laguna-phuket">Atrium</a>, Laguna: pool, gym, spa, restaurant or bar, security, parking, kids area. From €154,861</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/pu-condo-patong-phuket">Pu Condo</a>, Patong: pool, gym, spa, coworking, restaurant or bar, security, parking. From €105,209</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/ever-prime-residences-kata-phuket">Ever Prime Residences</a>, Kata: pool, gym, spa, security, parking, kids area, garden. From €104,779</li></ul><p><a href="/top/phuket?sort=facilities">Sort the Phuket Top 100 by facilities</a> or pick the ones you need, for example <a href="/top/phuket?amen=pool,gym">pool and gym</a>.</p><h2>Cheapest entry prices</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/phuket/arise-vibe-thalang-phuket">Arise Vibe</a>, Thalang: from €62,054, about €2,435 per m² (#74)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/aura-condominium-chalong-phuket">Aura Condominium</a>, Chalong: from €79,630, about €2,823 per m² (#37)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/next-point-condominium-naiharn-hills-phuket">Next Point Condominium</a>, Naiharn Hills: from €82,721, about €3,652 per m² (#19)</li><li><a href="/top/phuket/lavisco-living-nai-yang-phuket">Lavisco Living</a>, Nai Yang: from €84,559, about €3,336 per m² (#61)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/phuket?max=100000">All projects under €100,000 in Phuket</a></p><h2>How the scores work</h2><p>Every project gets ten scores from 0 to 100: price, value per m², location, beach, facilities, delivery, developer, space, visitor interest and listing detail. The grade of each project is the plain average of the ten. Price, value, space and visitor interest compare a project with the other 99 in the same ranking. Beach uses the straight-line distance to the coastline on OpenStreetMap, and facilities counts what the developer's public listing shows (pool, gym, spa, coworking, security and so on). Those two use the same scale in every destination, so you can compare Bali with Batumi.</p><p>None of this is advice to buy. Prices and dates come from public listings and change. Ask the developer before you decide, and check the facilities are built, not only drawn.</p>`},{slug:"best-new-builds-tulum-scored-2026",title:"Best new builds in Tulum, scored: price, beach and facilities (2026)",desc:"We scored the 100 projects in our Tulum Top 100 from 0 to 100 on price, beach, facilities and seven more things. Median price €146,927; 6 are within 1 km of the sea.",date:"2026-10-06",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/top/tulum/buongiorno-living-residence-aldea-zama-tulum-0.webp",tags:["rankings","tulum"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Almost everything for sale in Tulum is still being built. That makes the developer, the delivery date and the distance to the beach more important than the photos. We scored the 100 projects in our Tulum Top 100 on the same ten things.</p><p>The median project starts at €146,927, at about €2,835 per m². 9 of the 100 start under €100,000, 6 are within 1 km of the sea and 1 is ready to move in today. 98 list a pool, 13 a gym and 9 a coworking space.</p><h2>The top 5 overall</h2><ul><li>#1 <a href="/top/tulum/buongiorno-living-residence-aldea-zama-tulum">Buongiorno Living Residence</a>, Aldea Zama: from €105,614, grade 72/100, 2.2 km to the sea</li><li>#2 <a href="/top/tulum/regina-black-la-veleta-tulum">Regina Black</a>, La Veleta: from €80,949, grade 60/100, 4.0 km to the sea</li><li>#3 <a href="/top/tulum/ik-residences-la-veleta-tulum">IK Residences</a>, La Veleta: from €255,642, grade 60/100, 2.8 km to the sea</li><li>#4 <a href="/top/tulum/artem-by-dk-tulum-beach-tulum">Artem By DK</a>, Tulum Beach: from €240,798, grade 58/100, 800 m to the sea</li><li>#5 <a href="/top/tulum/brahma-aldea-zama-tulum">Brahma</a>, Aldea Zama: from €211,169, grade 57/100, 1.7 km to the sea</li></ul><h2>Closest to the sea</h2><p>Measured in a straight line to the coastline, so the walk is a bit longer.</p><ul><li><a href="/top/tulum/antal-tankah-tulum">ANTAL</a>, Tankah: 50 m, from €120,901 (#24)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/xiib-kaab-tulum-beach-tulum">Xiib Kaab</a>, Tulum Beach: 400 m, from €121,606 (#70)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/baikal-residences-akumal-tulum">Baikal Residences</a>, Akumal: 800 m, from €340,446 (#46)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/artem-by-dk-tulum-beach-tulum">Artem By DK</a>, Tulum Beach: 800 m, from €240,798 (#4)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/zatara-tulum-beach-tulum">Zatara</a>, Tulum Beach: 900 m, from €220,304 (#43)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/tulum?sort=sea">See all Tulum projects sorted by distance to the sea</a></p><h2>Best facilities</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/tulum/buongiorno-living-residence-aldea-zama-tulum">Buongiorno Living Residence</a>, Aldea Zama: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, parking, garden. From €105,614</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/seremonia-selva-la-veleta-tulum">Seremonia Selva</a>, La Veleta: pool, gym, spa, restaurant or bar, security, parking, kids area. From €161,386</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/regina-black-la-veleta-tulum">Regina Black</a>, La Veleta: pool, gym, spa, coworking, restaurant or bar, garden, lift. From €80,949</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/julia-by-dk-selvazama-tulum">Julia By DK</a>, Selvazama: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, restaurant or bar, yoga. From €160,451</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/artem-by-dk-tulum-beach-tulum">Artem By DK</a>, Tulum Beach: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, restaurant or bar, lift. From €240,798</li></ul><p><a href="/top/tulum?sort=facilities">Sort the Tulum Top 100 by facilities</a> or pick the ones you need, for example <a href="/top/tulum?amen=pool,gym">pool and gym</a>.</p><h2>Cheapest entry prices</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/tulum/casa-chichil-aldea-zama-tulum">Casa Chichil</a>, Aldea Zama: from €76,095, about €3,805 per m² (#57)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/natum-tulum-region-15-tulum">Natum Tulum</a>, Region 15: from €78,387, about €2,529 per m² (#84)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/regina-black-la-veleta-tulum">Regina Black</a>, La Veleta: from €80,949, about €2,453 per m² (#2)</li><li><a href="/top/tulum/yemaya-aldea-zama-tulum">YEMAYA</a>, Aldea Zama: from €83,910, about €3,108 per m² (#85)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/tulum?max=100000">All projects under €100,000 in Tulum</a></p><h2>How the scores work</h2><p>Every project gets ten scores from 0 to 100: price, value per m², location, beach, facilities, delivery, developer, space, visitor interest and listing detail. The grade of each project is the plain average of the ten. Price, value, space and visitor interest compare a project with the other 99 in the same ranking. Beach uses the straight-line distance to the coastline on OpenStreetMap, and facilities counts what the developer's public listing shows (pool, gym, spa, coworking, security and so on). Those two use the same scale in every destination, so you can compare Bali with Batumi.</p><p>None of this is advice to buy. Prices and dates come from public listings and change. Ask the developer before you decide, and check the facilities are built, not only drawn.</p>`},{slug:"best-new-builds-batumi-scored-2026",title:"Best new builds in Batumi, scored: price, beach and facilities (2026)",desc:"We scored the 100 projects in our Batumi Top 100 from 0 to 100 on price, beach, facilities and seven more things. Median price €48,630; 73 are within 1 km of the sea.",date:"2026-10-06",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/top/batumi/status-house-by-elt-building-batumi-boulevard-batumi-0.webp",tags:["rankings","batumi"],minutes:4,html:`<p>Batumi is the cheap one: most new apartments in our ranking cost less than a car in Western Europe, and most are a short walk from the Black Sea. We scored the 100 projects in our Batumi Top 100 on the same ten things.</p><p>The median project starts at €48,630, at about €1,358 per m². 88 of the 100 start under €100,000, 73 are within 1 km of the sea and 34 are ready to move in today. 56 list a pool, 44 a gym and 19 a coworking space.</p><h2>The top 5 overall</h2><ul><li>#1 <a href="/top/batumi/red-batumi-boulevard-batumi">Red</a>, Batumi Boulevard: from €35,067, grade 82/100, 500 m to the sea</li><li>#2 <a href="/top/batumi/status-house-by-elt-building-batumi-boulevard-batumi">Status House by Elt Building</a>, Batumi Boulevard: from €38,664, grade 79/100, 300 m to the sea</li><li>#3 <a href="/top/batumi/lemongarden-residence-spa-airport-batumi">LemonGarden Residence & Spa</a>, Airport district: from €35,264, grade 78/100, 600 m to the sea</li><li>#4 <a href="/top/batumi/ande-metropolis-airport-batumi">Ande Metropolis</a>, Airport district: from €36,495, grade 77/100, 400 m to the sea</li><li>#5 <a href="/top/batumi/palm-residence-airport-batumi">Palm Residence</a>, Airport district: from €43,490, grade 77/100, 100 m to the sea</li></ul><h2>Closest to the sea</h2><p>Measured in a straight line to the coastline, so the walk is a bit longer.</p><ul><li><a href="/top/batumi/sunway-suites-airport-batumi">Sunway Suites</a>, Airport district: 100 m, from €49,894 (#71)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/marina-club-airport-batumi">Marina Club</a>, Airport district: 100 m, from €75,326 (#54)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/portofino-batumi-boulevard-batumi">Portofino</a>, Batumi Boulevard: 100 m, from €67,719 (#86)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/aqua-hotel-apartments-airport-batumi">Aqua Hotel & Apartments</a>, Airport district: 100 m, from €41,269 (#44)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/palm-residence-airport-batumi">Palm Residence</a>, Airport district: 100 m, from €43,490 (#5)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/batumi?sort=sea">See all Batumi projects sorted by distance to the sea</a></p><h2>Best facilities</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/batumi/red-batumi-boulevard-batumi">Red</a>, Batumi Boulevard: pool, gym, spa, rooftop, restaurant or bar, security, parking. From €35,067</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/lemongarden-residence-spa-airport-batumi">LemonGarden Residence & Spa</a>, Airport district: pool, gym, spa, restaurant or bar, security, parking, kids area. From €35,264</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/palm-residence-airport-batumi">Palm Residence</a>, Airport district: pool, gym, spa, restaurant or bar, security, parking, kids area. From €43,490</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/skylux-batumi-residence-batumi-boulevard-batumi">SkyLux Batumi Residence</a>, Batumi Boulevard: pool, gym, spa, rooftop, restaurant or bar, security, parking. From €46,629</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/bana-development-makhinjauri-batumi">Bana Development</a>, Makhinjauri: pool, gym, spa, coworking, rooftop, restaurant or bar, security. From €37,368</li></ul><p><a href="/top/batumi?sort=facilities">Sort the Batumi Top 100 by facilities</a> or pick the ones you need, for example <a href="/top/batumi?amen=pool,gym">pool and gym</a>.</p><h2>Cheapest entry prices</h2><ul><li><a href="/top/batumi/semira-tolia-airport-batumi">Semira Tolia</a>, Airport district: from €19,965, about €661 per m² (#34)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/maisi-batumi-batumi">Maisi</a>, Batumi centre: from €26,422, about €855 per m² (#90)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/tbel-abuseridze-st-24a-batumi-batumi">Comfort Palace · Tbel Abuseridze St, 24a</a>, Batumi centre: from €26,666, about €1,058 per m² (#88)</li><li><a href="/top/batumi/angisa-development-angisa-st-47-airport-batumi">Angisa Development · Angisa St, 47</a>, Airport district: from €27,327, about €970 per m² (#43)</li></ul><p><a href="/top/batumi?max=100000">All projects under €100,000 in Batumi</a></p><h2>How the scores work</h2><p>Every project gets ten scores from 0 to 100: price, value per m², location, beach, facilities, delivery, developer, space, visitor interest and listing detail. The grade of each project is the plain average of the ten. Price, value, space and visitor interest compare a project with the other 99 in the same ranking. Beach uses the straight-line distance to the coastline on OpenStreetMap, and facilities counts what the developer's public listing shows (pool, gym, spa, coworking, security and so on). Those two use the same scale in every destination, so you can compare Bali with Batumi.</p><p>None of this is advice to buy. Prices and dates come from public listings and change. Ask the developer before you decide, and check the facilities are built, not only drawn.</p>`},{slug:"what-50000-euros-buys-2026",title:"What €50,000 Buys in 2026: 140 Checked Homes, Country by Country",desc:"We sorted every home on RealHunt by price. 46 of 140 cost €50,000 or less, and almost all of them are in three countries.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/DcoUnwBDDxF-1.webp",tags:["data","prices"],minutes:3,html:`<p>€50,000 is roughly what a new mid-size car costs, or a deposit on a flat in most European cities. We wanted to know what it buys if you spend it on a whole house instead, so we sorted every home currently published on RealHunt by price.</p>
<p>The short answer: 46 of the 140 homes on the site cost €50,000 or less. They are not spread evenly. 29 are in Italy, 9 in Japan and 6 in Sweden. France and Denmark have one each. Spain, the United States, Canada and Mexico have none under that line.</p>
<p>Every home here passed the same check: a person looked at the photos and the listing, and anything that needs a new roof, is a ruin, is only land or has a single photo was left out. So these are cheap homes you could sleep in, not projects.</p>
<h2>Italy: the most homes for the money</h2>
<p>Italy has the cheapest home on the site, a stone house in a Lazio hill town for €12,000, about 55 minutes from Rome's Ciampino airport. Half of the 39 Italian homes cost €41,000 or less. At this price you are mostly buying in small inland villages: Tuscany, Lazio, Umbria and Abruzzo come up again and again. Expect thick stone walls, a small kitchen, and a piazza five minutes away on foot.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DcoUnwBDDxF"><img src="/finds/DcoUnwBDDxF-1.webp" alt="Stone house in a hill town 55 min from Rome" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€12,000</strong> · Stone house in a hill town 55 min from Rome<span>Lazio, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/grotte-santo-stefano-13000"><img src="/finds/grotte-santo-stefano-13000.webp" alt="Stone-village home near Viterbo, 1h30 from Rome" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€13,000</strong> · Stone-village home near Viterbo, 1h30 from Rome<span>Grotte Santo Stefano, Viterbo, Lazio, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdSm-JkiJem"><img src="/finds/DdSm-JkiJem-1.webp" alt="Two-bedroom apartment with terrace in a historic Tuscan village" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€16,000</strong> · Two-bedroom apartment with terrace in a historic Tuscan village<span>Montieri, Grosseto, Tuscany, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<p>What the price hides: Italy's buying costs are the highest on our list. For a second home, registration tax is 9% of the cadastral value (a tax value that is usually well below the price), and the notary and registry add about €2,500. Our cost estimate adds roughly 10% plus €2,500 to be safe, so a €30,000 house is closer to €35,500 all in.</p>
<h2>Japan: bigger houses, lower running costs</h2>
<p>Japan's cheapest homes on RealHunt start at €18,400 and the middle of the list is €51,980. 9 of 18 are under €50,000. For that money the houses tend to be larger than in Italy, often with a garden, and many are near the coast in Wakayama, Kyoto prefecture and Okayama. Foreigners can own land and houses outright in Japan, with no residency needed to buy.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DbbD15oDKLz"><img src="/finds/DbbD15oDKLz-1.webp" alt="Japanese home with a sakura garden" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€19,320</strong> · Japanese home with a sakura garden<span>Usuki, Oita, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdJ6Zs_DcnX"><img src="/finds/DdJ6Zs_DcnX-1.webp" alt="Single-storey Japanese house in a seaside town" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€34,960</strong> · Single-storey Japanese house in a seaside town<span>Kainan, Wakayama, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdWPFmBkdhr"><img src="/finds/DdWPFmBkdhr-1.webp" alt="Single-storey house with a huge lawn on the Seto Inland Sea" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€43,240</strong> · Single-storey house with a huge lawn on the Seto Inland Sea<span>Mitoyo, Kagawa, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>Sweden: where €50,000 is the entry ticket</h2>
<p>Sweden's median on our list is €72,000, higher than Italy or Japan, and only 6 of 41 Swedish homes are under €50,000. But what you get is often a detached wooden house with a big plot, near a lake, with a wood stove. Anyone can buy in Sweden, and the only real buying cost is a 1.5% stamp duty. There is no notary.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dc6j54PF6hV"><img src="/finds/Dc6j54PF6hV-1.webp" alt="Charming wooden house in the forest near a lake" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€22,000</strong> · Charming wooden house in the forest near a lake<span>Central Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdPBLT2CLlb"><img src="/finds/DdPBLT2CLlb-1.webp" alt="Cosy villa with sauna, 100 m from the river" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€25,960</strong> · Cosy villa with sauna, 100 m from the river<span>Northern Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dc2FknAIzJt"><img src="/finds/Dc2FknAIzJt-1.webp" alt="Cosy Dalarna cottage with guest cottage and pergola" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€36,000</strong> · Cosy Dalarna cottage with guest cottage and pergola<span>Stensbo, Dalarna, Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>North America starts higher</h2>
<p>In the United States the cheapest move-in-ready home we have published is €65,428 (a brick row house in McKees Rocks, Pennsylvania, asking $75,000). Canada starts at €55,842 and Mexico at €50,575. Those markets have plenty of cheaper listings, but at the very bottom many are sold as investor projects: boarded windows, fire damage, or a plot with a trailer on it. They do not pass our quality rule.</p>
<h2>What we take from this</h2>
<p>If €50,000 is your hard ceiling, Italy is where you will have the most choice by far, Japan is where you get the most house, and Sweden is where you get the most land. Add 2% (Sweden) to 10% (Italy) on top for taxes and fees, and always pay for a survey before you sign.</p>
<p>You can see every home under €50,000 on one page: <a href="/cheap-houses/under-50000">houses for sale under €50,000</a>, or just <a href="/cheap-houses/under-30000">the ones under €30,000</a>.</p>`},{slug:"months-of-rent-vs-buying-a-house",title:"How Many Months of Rent Would Buy You a Whole House?",desc:"We divided the median price of homes on RealHunt by three monthly rents. At €1,200 a month, a typical Italian village house costs less than three years of rent.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/DdSm-JkiJem-1.webp",tags:["data","rent"],minutes:3,html:`<p>This is the comparison that surprises people most. If you pay €1,200 a month in rent, you hand over €14,400 a year. In three years that is €43,200, which is more than the median price of an Italian home on our site (€41,000).</p>
<p>That does not mean you should quit your lease tomorrow. A house in a village two hours from the nearest city is not the same thing as a flat near your job. But the comparison is useful because it puts a price you cannot picture (a house in another country) next to one you pay every month.</p>
<h2>The table</h2>
<p>We took the median price of the homes published on RealHunt in each country and divided it by three monthly rents: €800, €1,200 and €2,000. We did not use official rent figures for any city. Pick the column closest to what you pay.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Median home on RealHunt</th><th>Months at €800</th><th>Months at €1,200</th><th>Months at €2,000</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Italy</td><td>€41,000</td><td>51</td><td>34</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>€51,980</td><td>65</td><td>43</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>Sweden</td><td>€72,000</td><td>90</td><td>60</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Mexico</td><td>€80,920</td><td>101</td><td>67</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>United States</td><td>€85,929</td><td>107</td><td>72</td><td>43</td></tr><tr><td>Canada</td><td>€92,552</td><td>116</td><td>77</td><td>46</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Across all 140 homes the median is €61,280. At €1,200 a month that is 51 months of rent, a little over four years.</p>
<h2>What the table leaves out</h2>
<p>Buying costs come first. They range from about 2% of the price in Sweden to about 10% in Italy, plus fixed fees. On each home page we show the estimate for that exact price.</p>
<p>Then there is the cost of keeping a house. Council tax on a small Italian village home is often a few hundred euros a year. In Sweden and Canada, heating in winter is the big one. In the US, property tax is set by the county and is usually shown in the listing. None of this is close to rent, but it is not zero.</p>
<p>And there is the part no spreadsheet captures: whether you actually want to live there. The cheapest homes are cheap because people left those places. Some are coming back to life with remote workers and retirees; others are quiet for a reason. Visit before you buy. Stay a week, in winter if you can.</p>
<h2>Try it with your own rent</h2>
<p>On every home page on RealHunt there is a line that says how many months of rent the house is worth. It uses a rough average rent for the city your browser is in. It is a nudge, not financial advice, but it is a fair way to see these prices.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdSm-JkiJem"><img src="/finds/DdSm-JkiJem-1.webp" alt="Two-bedroom apartment with terrace in a historic Tuscan village" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€16,000</strong> · Two-bedroom apartment with terrace in a historic Tuscan village<span>Montieri, Grosseto, Tuscany, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dcsm9sZoDMp"><img src="/finds/Dcsm9sZoDMp-1.webp" alt="Two-bedroom apartment with two balconies in a historic village" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€16,000</strong> · Two-bedroom apartment with two balconies in a historic village<span>Fiamignano, Rieti, Lazio, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DbbD15oDKLz"><img src="/finds/DbbD15oDKLz-1.webp" alt="Japanese home with a sakura garden" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€19,320</strong> · Japanese home with a sakura garden<span>Usuki, Oita, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<p>Start with the cheapest: <a href="/cheap-houses/under-30000">homes under €30,000</a>.</p>`},{slug:"cheap-houses-sweden-under-100000",title:"Cheap Houses in Sweden: What €22,000 to €99,000 Gets You",desc:"41 Swedish homes under €100,000, most of them red wooden houses on big plots near a lake. Where they are, what they cost to buy, and what to check.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/DbjsJpRGdDa-1.webp",tags:["sweden","guide"],minutes:3,html:`<p>Sweden is the country people are most surprised by when they browse RealHunt. It has a reputation for being expensive, and in Stockholm it is. But 41 of the homes on our site are Swedish, all under €100,000, and most of them look like the picture you have in your head: a red wooden house with white corners, a garden, and a lake somewhere behind the trees.</p>
<figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DbjsJpRGdDa"><img src="/finds/DbjsJpRGdDa-1.webp" alt="Classic red Swedish house with gardens and berry bushes" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€57,000</strong> · Classic red Swedish house with gardens and berry bushes<span>Nordmark, Värmland, Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure>
<h2>Where the cheap ones are</h2>
<p>Värmland, in the west near the Norwegian border, has the most homes on our list. After that come Småland, Dalarna, Skåne and Jönköping. These are forest and lake regions with small towns, not suburbs. Many houses are an hour or so from an airport: Karlstad, Växjö, Jönköping or Göteborg depending on where you are.</p>
<p>The cheapest Swedish home on the site is €22,000, a wooden house in the forest 550 metres from a lake. The median is €72,000. For that you usually get a detached house with a plot measured in thousands of square metres, not hundreds. Several come with a sauna, a guest cabin or a barn.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dc6j54PF6hV"><img src="/finds/Dc6j54PF6hV-1.webp" alt="Charming wooden house in the forest near a lake" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€22,000</strong> · Charming wooden house in the forest near a lake<span>Central Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DccwxoxINB2"><img src="/finds/DccwxoxINB2-1.webp" alt="Off-grid lakefront cottage with own jetty and wood-fired sauna" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€72,000</strong> · Off-grid lakefront cottage with own jetty and wood-fired sauna<span>Sunnemo, Värmland, Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DdP0Csyilzz"><img src="/finds/DdP0Csyilzz-1.webp" alt="Sea-view cabin with swimming access and boat mooring" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€64,000</strong> · Sea-view cabin with swimming access and boat mooring<span>Hornön, Västernorrland, Sweden</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>How buying works</h2>
<p>This is where Sweden is easy. Anyone can buy a house, whether or not you live in the EU. There is no notary. The buyer pays a stamp duty (lagfart) of 1.5% of the price plus a small fixed fee to register the title, and a mortgage registration fee if you borrow. The seller pays the estate agent. On a €60,000 house your buying costs are roughly €1,000 to €2,000.</p>
<p>Sales usually run as open bidding through the agent. The asking price is a starting point, and popular houses can go above it. You sign a contract, pay a deposit of around 10%, and the rest on the day you get the keys.</p>
<h2>What to check before you bid</h2>
<p>Winter. Ask what heating the house has (wood, electric, a heat pump or geothermal) and what it cost to heat last winter. Several homes on our list mention geothermal or heat pumps, which bring the bills down a lot.</p>
<p>Water and drains. Country houses often have their own well and septic system. Ask when the septic tank was last emptied and whether the well water has been tested.</p>
<p>Access. A gravel road that is lovely in June can be hard work in January. Ask who clears the snow.</p>
<p>In Sweden the buyer is expected to inspect the house properly before buying, and hidden faults you could have found are usually your problem afterwards. Many sellers order an inspection report; read it, even with a translator, and consider your own.</p>
<h2>Browse them</h2>
<p>See <a href="/cheap-houses/cabins-and-cottages-in-sweden">cabins and cottages in Sweden</a>, <a href="/cheap-houses/near-a-lake-in-sweden">Swedish homes near a lake</a>, or <a href="/cheap-houses/with-sauna">homes with a sauna</a>.</p>`},{slug:"cheap-houses-japan-move-in-ready",title:"Not Every Cheap House in Japan Is an Abandoned Akiya",desc:"Stories about free houses in Japan usually show wrecks. These 18 homes start at €18,400 and you could move into them.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/Da7vZIFDIuo-1.webp",tags:["japan","guide"],minutes:3,html:`<p>You have probably seen the headlines: Japan has millions of empty houses, and some towns give them away. It is true. It is also why so many people are disappointed when they look closer. Most of the free and near-free houses need a new roof, new plumbing and months of work, and some are not worth saving.</p>
<p>So we set a different bar. The 18 Japanese homes on RealHunt all look like somewhere you could live now. Prices start at €18,400 and the middle of the list is €51,980.</p>
<figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Da7vZIFDIuo"><img src="/finds/Da7vZIFDIuo-1.webp" alt="Hillside kominka with sea views over the Kyoto coast" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€54,280</strong> · Hillside kominka with sea views over the Kyoto coast<span>Maizuru, Kyoto, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure>
<h2>What the money buys</h2>
<p>At the bottom of the range you get older wooden houses in the countryside: a 100-year-old farmhouse with its own farmland, or a 1977 single-storey house with a sakura garden in Usuki, Oita, about 500 metres from the sea. Around €40,000 to €60,000 the houses are often renovated, with new kitchens and bathrooms, and several have sea views on the Seto Inland Sea or the Sea of Japan coast.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DbbD15oDKLz"><img src="/finds/DbbD15oDKLz-1.webp" alt="Japanese home with a sakura garden" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€19,320</strong> · Japanese home with a sakura garden<span>Usuki, Oita, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/DcTSbXtj9qu"><img src="/finds/DcTSbXtj9qu-1.webp" alt="Freshly renovated five-bedroom house with an ocean view" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€56,120</strong> · Freshly renovated five-bedroom house with an ocean view<span>Kashiwazaki, Niigata, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Db59YYrjN5h"><img src="/finds/Db59YYrjN5h-1.webp" alt="Renovated island house with one of Japan&#x27;s best sea views" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€57,040</strong> · Renovated island house with one of Japan&#x27;s best sea views<span>Kojima, Okayama, Japan</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<p>At the top, around €91,000, there is a Japanese-style single-storey house with an engawa (the wooden veranda) and a garden in Yugawara, a coastal hot spring town about 90 minutes from Tokyo.</p>
<h2>Can a foreigner buy?</h2>
<p>Yes. Japan lets foreigners own houses and land outright, with no visa or residency needed to buy. Buying costs add up to roughly 8% on top of the price: acquisition tax, registration, stamp duty and the agent's commission, which is capped by law. Owning a house does not give you a visa, so if you want to live there full time that is a separate question.</p>
<h2>What to check</h2>
<p>Age matters in Japan in a way it does not in Europe. Houses built before June 1981 were built to older earthquake rules. Many are fine and some have been reinforced, but ask. Ask too about the septic system, whether the land is freehold or leased, and what the fixed asset tax is each year (usually low for older rural houses).</p>
<p>Distance is the other thing. A house in a quiet coastal town can be two or three hours from a big city by train. That is the point for some buyers and a problem for others.</p>
<h2>Browse them</h2>
<p><a href="/cheap-houses/by-the-sea-in-japan">Japanese homes near the sea</a> · <a href="/cheap-houses/under-50000-in-japan">Japan under €50,000</a> · <a href="/homes/japan">all homes in Japan</a></p>`},{slug:"italy-houses-by-the-sea-under-50000",title:"Italian Homes Near the Sea for Under €50,000: Where They Actually Are",desc:"Cheap Italian houses are usually in inland hill towns. A few are close to the water. Here is where they are and what the catch is.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/acquappesa-sea-view-39000.webp",tags:["italy","sea"],minutes:3,html:`<p>Most of the cheap Italian homes you see online are in inland villages, and there is a reason: the coast is where Italians go on holiday, so that is where prices are high. But not everywhere. On RealHunt right now there are a handful of homes under €50,000 that are on the coast or a few minutes' drive from a beach, and they cluster in three places: Calabria, Sicily and Puglia.</p>
<figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/acquappesa-sea-view-39000"><img src="/finds/acquappesa-sea-view-39000.webp" alt="Sea-view terrace in Acquappesa, Calabria" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€39,000</strong> · Sea-view terrace in Acquappesa, Calabria<span>Acquappesa, Cosenza, Calabria, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure>
<h2>Calabria</h2>
<p>The toe of Italy has the longest coastline of any mainland region and some of the lowest prices. In Acquappesa, on the Tyrrhenian side, there is a one-bedroom place with a sea-view terrace for €39,000, about 2 km from the water. Further south in Falconara Albanese there is a three-room flat with a sea view for €41,000. Two more homes in Calabria, at €30,000 and €35,000, are listed as five minutes from the beach.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/falconara-albanese-41000"><img src="/finds/idl-36848163-0.webp" alt="Apartment with sea view in Falconara Albanese, Cosenza" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€41,000</strong> · Apartment with sea view in Falconara Albanese, Cosenza<span>Falconara Albanese, Cosenza, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dcgta8yjCAR"><img src="/finds/Dcgta8yjCAR-1.webp" alt="Sea-view house five minutes from the beach" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€30,000</strong> · Sea-view house five minutes from the beach<span>Calabria, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dc32k2tDKLf"><img src="/finds/Dc32k2tDKLf-1.webp" alt="Sea-view house five minutes from the beach" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€35,000</strong> · Sea-view house five minutes from the beach<span>Calabria, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>Sicily</h2>
<p>In Marsala, on the west coast, a 135 m² apartment is €34,000, about 600 metres from the sea. That is €252 per square metre, the lowest on the site for an Italian home with a size in the listing. In Acquedolci, on the north coast near Messina, a two-level flat that was largely renovated is €49,000. There is also a sea-view home with an 87 m² panoramic terrace 20 minutes from Messina for the same price.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/marsala-34000"><img src="/finds/idl-36884835-0.webp" alt="Apartment in Marsala, Trapani" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€34,000</strong> · Apartment in Marsala, Trapani<span>Marsala, Trapani, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/acquedolci-49000"><img src="/finds/idl-34296298-0.webp" alt="Duplex in Acquedolci, Messina" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€49,000</strong> · Duplex in Acquedolci, Messina<span>Acquedolci, Messina, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dclv5CJDdhi"><img src="/finds/Dclv5CJDdhi-1.webp" alt="Sea-view home with a panoramic terrace" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€49,000</strong> · Sea-view home with a panoramic terrace<span>Sicily, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>Puglia and Sardinia</h2>
<p>Puglia has two whitewashed and vaulted houses around 20 to 25 minutes from the beach at €28,000 and €49,000. In Sardinia, a furnished tower house on four floors in a historic district is €35,000, about 3 km from the beaches.</p>
<h2>The catch</h2>
<p>These are mostly apartments or small village houses, not villas. Sea views at this price usually come with stairs, a small kitchen and a building that has seen a few decades. Summer is busy and some coastal towns are very quiet from November to March. And the costs of buying in Italy are high: plan for 10% on top, plus about €2,500 in fees.</p>
<p>Always ask for the APE (the energy certificate) and whether the property is "conforme": that what is built matches what is registered at the land registry. A good local geometra can check this for a few hundred euros, and it is worth every one.</p>
<p>All of them in one place: <a href="/cheap-houses/by-the-sea-in-italy">cheap houses by the sea in Italy</a>. For the hill towns, see <a href="/cheap-houses/stone-houses-in-italy">stone houses in Italy</a>.</p>`},{slug:"move-in-ready-houses-under-100000-usa",title:"Move-In-Ready Houses in the US for Around $100,000",desc:"We published 13 US homes that cost under €100,000 and do not need gutting. They start at $75,000. Here is where they are.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/mckees-rocks-65428-0.webp",tags:["usa","guide"],minutes:3,html:`<p>Search for cheap houses in the US and you find thousands under $100,000. Open them and many are the same story: fire damage, boarded windows, "cash only, investor special". We went through them looking for the opposite, homes you could move into this month, and published 13 so far.</p>
<p>They are asking between $75,000 and $110,000. On RealHunt every price is in euros so homes in different countries can be compared; at the rate we used, that is €65,428 to €95,961.</p>
<figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/mckees-rocks-65428"><img src="/finds/mckees-rocks-65428-0.webp" alt="Updated 3-bed townhouse in McKees Rocks, Pennsylvania" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€65,428</strong> · Updated 3-bed townhouse in McKees Rocks, Pennsylvania<span>McKees Rocks, Pennsylvania</span></figcaption></a></figure>
<h2>Where they are</h2>
<p>The Pittsburgh area comes up more than anywhere else. McKees Rocks, McKeesport and Clairton, old steel towns along the rivers, have brick houses from the early 1900s with updated kitchens for $75,000 to $98,500. West Virginia has two: a three-bedroom on a double lot in Huntington for $89,900, and a two-bedroom with a newer metal roof in Chapmanville for $95,000. Then Indiana, Ohio, Oklahoma, Arkansas, Texas, New York, and one condo near the beach in Little River, South Carolina.</p>
<div class="rh-homes"><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/huntington-78426"><img src="/finds/huntington-78426-0.webp" alt="Updated 3-bed house in Huntington, West Virginia" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€78,426</strong> · Updated 3-bed house in Huntington, West Virginia<span>Huntington, West Virginia</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/chapmanville-82875"><img src="/finds/chapmanville-82875-0.webp" alt="Move-in ready 2-bed house in Chapmanville, West Virginia" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€82,875</strong> · Move-in ready 2-bed house in Chapmanville, West Virginia<span>Chapmanville, West Virginia</span></figcaption></a></figure><figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/clairton-85929"><img src="/finds/clairton-85929-0.webp" alt="Updated 3-bed house in Clairton, Pennsylvania" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€85,929</strong> · Updated 3-bed house in Clairton, Pennsylvania<span>Clairton, Pennsylvania</span></figcaption></a></figure></div>
<h2>What "move-in ready" means here</h2>
<p>We only publish a US home if the photos show a kitchen and bathroom in working order, floors, walls and windows in good shape, and no sign of major damage. It is not an inspection. It is a first filter so you do not waste an afternoon on a house with a hole in the roof.</p>
<h2>Buying as a foreigner</h2>
<p>Anyone can buy a house in the US. You do not need residency or a visa. Buyer closing costs are usually 2% to 5% of the price: title insurance, escrow and recording fees. Property tax is charged every year by the county or town, often somewhere between 0.5% and 2% of the assessed value, and the listing usually shows it. Owning a home does not give you the right to live in the US; that is a separate visa question.</p>
<h2>What to check</h2>
<p>Get a home inspection. Ask about the age of the roof, the furnace and the water heater, which are the big bills in the first years. Check flood zone maps for houses near rivers. And look at the street on a map: in some of these towns, prices can change a lot from one block to the next.</p>
<p>See them all: <a href="/homes/united-states">homes in the United States</a>.</p>`},{slug:"how-we-choose-homes",title:"Why We Say No to Most Cheap Houses",desc:"A cheap house that needs €80,000 of work is not a cheap house. The rule we use to decide what goes on RealHunt, and why we turn most listings down.",date:"2026-09-23",author:"RealHunt Research",image:"/finds/Dc8Edq5j8H_-1.webp",tags:["method"],minutes:3,html:`<p>The internet is full of cheap houses. Most of them are cheap for a reason. The €1 houses in Italian villages come with an obligation to renovate, usually within three years, and the renovation costs far more than €1. The Japanese akiya you see in videos often need new roofs. And a lot of the American homes under $50,000 are listed for investors because they are not fit to live in.</p>
<p>RealHunt is for a different question: what can you buy for under €100,000 and live in, more or less as it is?</p>
<h2>The rule</h2>
<p>Every home is looked at by a person before it goes up. We say no to:</p>
<ul><li>homes that clearly need major work: a new roof, rebuilt walls, no kitchen or bathroom</li><li>ruins and shells, however pretty the stone</li><li>land on its own, or land with a trailer or a shed</li><li>listings with one photo, or photos of the town instead of the house</li><li>anything over €100,000</li></ul>
<p>Old is fine. Some of our favourite homes were built in the 1800s. What matters is that someone has looked after them.</p>
<p>In practice this means we turn down far more than we publish. We turn down about three listings for every one we publish.</p>
<figure class="rh-home"><a href="/deal/Dc8Edq5j8H_"><img src="/finds/Dc8Edq5j8H_-1.webp" alt="Fully renovated, fully furnished stone home in a quiet Tuscan village" loading="lazy" width="640" height="480"><figcaption><strong>€75,000</strong> · Fully renovated, fully furnished stone home in a quiet Tuscan village<span>Subbiano, Arezzo, Tuscany, Italy</span></figcaption></a></figure>
<h2>Where the homes come from</h2>
<p>Some are found by our own research on public listing sites. Some come from people and accounts who post homes they have found, and every one goes through the same check. Signed-in members can open the original listing and contact the seller or agent directly. RealHunt is not an estate agent and does not sell the houses.</p>
<h2>How the ranking works</h2>
<p>Every home that passes gets a grade from 70 to 98, built from 11 sub-scores: price, price per m², space, condition, extras, water, weather, airport, buying costs, listing detail and buyer interest. The front page, the country pages and the price lists are ordered by that grade, best first. Votes and saves only move the buyer-interest sub-score. <a href="/method">How the score works</a>. There are no paid placements in the ranking. Where there is a sponsored card, it says so.</p>
<h2>What we get wrong</h2>
<p>Photos can flatter. Listings go out of date. A house that looks fine may have a problem only a survey will find. If you see a home that is sold or not what it seems, use the report button on its page and we will check it. And before you buy anything, anywhere, get an independent inspection and a local lawyer or notary.</p>
<p><a href="/">See the best homes</a></p>`,updated:"2026-10-23"}];export{e as default};
